# {{KIT_NAME}}

One sentence describing a requirement goes in; a working {{DS_NAME}} frontend comes out. That is the entire purpose.

This file is the canonical instruction set for every agent working in this repository (Claude Code,
Cursor, Copilot, Codex, and others). `CLAUDE.md` only imports it. Do not duplicate content elsewhere.

## Before every UI task

Read `.claude/skills/{{DS_SLUG}}/SKILL.md`. It describes the design system at a conceptual
level—page anatomy, layout, tokens, component selection. The adjacent `craft.md` covers
DS-independent craft (states, motion, anti-patterns), and `pitfalls.md` lists everything that has
already gone wrong once in this kit. Exact component APIs come from `design/components-meta.json`
and the source code under `src/components/`, never from memory.

There is deliberately only one skill. Missing knowledge belongs there, not in a second one.
The skill stays under 500 lines and links at most one level of reference files next to it;
`pitfalls.md` is that one level and grows freely—add a pitfall there, not to the skill body.

## Structure

Vite + React + TypeScript + Tailwind, a single package, no workspace.

```
src/prototypes/<slug>/*.tsx    prototypes—create a file and its route exists (/p/<slug>)
src/prototypes/<slug>/_shared  shared shell/mock data for a prototype (not routed)
src/prototypes/<slug>/_shared/meta.ts  optional: title, description, judgeAt ('desktop' | 'mobile' | px), reference
src/router.tsx                 automatic routing; do not modify
src/HomeView.tsx               automatic overview of all prototypes
src/components/                the {{DS_NAME}} components—use them, do not reinvent them
design/tokens.css              frozen tokens—never regenerate; edit only deliberately
design/tokens.json             the same tokens as DTCG with provenance (source of every value)
design/components-meta.json    API source of truth: all components with props/variants/status
design/fixes.css               documented small corrections
design/kit.json                provenance stamp: which generator template built this kit
.claude/skills/{{DS_SLUG}}/    the skill (+ craft.md, pitfalls.md)
```

The scaffold files (`src/router.tsx`, `src/prototypes.ts`, `src/HomeView.tsx`, `src/main.tsx`,
`src/kit.css`, `src/viewport.ts`, `src/DeviceFrame.tsx`, `scripts/`, `vite.config.ts`, `tsconfig.json`) are generator-owned and get refreshed from the
generator; do not customize them—kit-specific styling belongs in `src/styles.css` and `design/`.

## Commands

```bash
pnpm dev                    # Port {{PORT}} (runs pnpm preflight first: Node, pnpm, deps, port)
pnpm typecheck
pnpm export                 # Static single-file export of all prototypes to export/index.html
pnpm export --only <slug>   # One prototype as a single file, for sharing
```

If a development server for this kit is already running on the port, reuse it. Otherwise, start
your own and stop only your own process (by PID) when finished. Never terminate processes owned by
others, and never use `pkill` by name.

## Modes

- **proto** (default): build a specific idea as a clickable prototype. If the request
  describes a flow, build and link multiple screens.
- **ideate**: work divergently—create 3–5 deliberately distinct directions for a question as
  rough standalone screens under `src/prototypes/idea-<topic>-<variant>/` (keep the topic short and
  identical across all variants so the home page groups them). Use little detail and maximize the
  difference between approaches. State each direction's hypothesis in one sentence in the
  file-header comment. "Done means" applies in a reduced form: typecheck, console, and tokens still
  matter; complete states and flows only where they support the idea. The header/footer shell is
  optional; an unframed content screen is enough if the idea does not depend on its surroundings.
  The best direction is then developed into a complete prototype.

## Working approach

Do not ask what exactly the user means—build. Make assumptions visible in the prototype and
state them afterward instead of clarifying them first. The prototype is the question, not the answer.

Use realistic content: real domain terminology, plausible numbers, no Lorem Ipsum, and no
`Item 1/Item 2` lists. The prototype is judged by its content, not by its layout.

If the user hands over licensed brand assets (fonts, logos, icons), use them exactly as delivered
and replace any substitute the kit shipped with; note the swap at the top of `design/fonts.css`.

## Done means

1. `pnpm typecheck` passes.
2. Checked in a browser **at the width the prototype targets**, with no console errors. Default:
   desktop at 1280px or wider. If the brief is mobile-first or names a device, set `judgeAt:
   'mobile'` in `_shared/meta.ts` and also check at 375px—open `/#/frame/p/<slug>` or use the
   overview's Auto / Desktop / Mobile toggle (Auto follows `judgeAt`).
3. No hardcoded color or spacing value in the diff—everything comes from tokens.
4. Every flow has been clicked through once; empty, loading, and error states exist wherever they
   would exist in the real product.
5. Anything you had to work around is recorded in `.claude/skills/{{DS_SLUG}}/pitfalls.md`.
